      "text": "Further research is needed to ascertain whether the high brine and residual cosolvent contents of residual still bottoms precludes effective PFAS destruction (as is the case for electrochemical oxidation; [28] , however this concern may be mitigated by emerging methods to recover the salt brine, which would further benefit treatment operations by reducing costs and emissions from salt inputs. By coupling effective and recov - erable concentration technologies like regenerable AERs with resilient destructive technologies, future hybrid remediation trains can eliminate entirely the need for offsite transport and disposal of PFAS wastes. 5. Conclusions This work provides an evaluation of the feasibility of regenerating PFAS-laden anion exchange resins for treatment of AFFF-impacted groundwater using both regenerable resins and PFAS-selective resins intended for single-use operation. Results provide insights into the ef - ficacy of AER regeneration, optimization of regenerant solution mix - tures, potential on-site cosolvent recovery, and the overall sustainability of implementing AER systems with regeneration schemes. The following conclusions can be made: \u2022 \u2018PFAS-selective \u2019 resins intended for single-use operation can be practically regenerated using a mixture of salt brine and organic cosolvent, as is the case for conventionally regenerable resins. \u2022 Regeneration of PFAS-selective AERs requires higher cosolvent fractions and more BVs than regenerable resins, with 90 % methanol"
